3704,-0.014080819673836231,-7.033323527139146e-06,0.0,-0.002319504739716649,-0.024393649771809578,-0.04507935419678688,-0.008508975617587566,-3.2186455882765586e-06,-0.9244973659515381,-0.34001556038856506,-0.00268503837287426,-0.0007169060409069061,-0.0004259632551111281,-4.768360213347478e-06,0.0,-2.4318398573086597e-05,-3.576278118089249e-07,-0.018859008327126503,-0.6771270036697388,-1.7458882331848145,-0.7818419933319092,-0.00012385078298393637,-0.5007160902023315,-6.0794889577664435e-05,-0.6185320019721985,-0.05522145330905914,-7.510157047363464e-06,-0.00018094333063345402,-0.07139842957258224,-2.3841830625315197e-06,-0.0645914301276207,-7.152555099310121e-07,-2.3841830625315197e-06,-7.152555099310121e-07,-2.145764938177308e-06,-3.540453326422721e-05,-4.768370445162873e-07,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-1.7881377516459906e-06,-1.4543427823809907e-05,-1.4305012882687151e-05,-3.099436753473128e-06,-1.9073468138230965e-06,-9.536738616588991e-07,-2.5987286790041253e-05,-0.07572959363460541,-0.6598446369171143,-0.018979258835315704,-3.2186455882765586e-06,-0.0032879835925996304,-0.06106299161911011,-8.940656698541716e-06,-0.09117398411035538,-0.00614503538236022,-2.50339189733495e-06,-0.0007798014557920396,-2.3841574147809297e-05],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,57960,10374,13233,90,64,2137,3,57960,10374,13233,90,65,2137,3,323,57960,10374,13233,90,66,31716,387,2326,4982,22879,11,1741,429,279,9210,1948,894,315,1105,374,279,29783,9210,57960,15976,2418,220,576,8123,315,279,27853,32507,291,2248,7907,553,1493,2326,22879,374,57960,37018,90,16,15170,59,26888,90,18,21,15,3417,2418,220,7379,198,78045,18,1124,9407,61,17,1124,15976,481,220,17,1124,9407,61,18,1124,15976,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ be three unit vectors, such that the angle between any of them is the acute angle $\\theta.$  The volume of the tetrahedron generated by these three vectors is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}.$  Find\n\\[3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"50a23365e77b7b315009d3f47fd85e1dc6a5cfe6a69ce0b811c9d95880bce246"},"seconds":79.21591258049011,"seed":585562604120551299,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to find the value of \\(3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta\\) given that the volume of the tetrahedron generated by three unit vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) is \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}\\), and the angle between any two of these vectors is \\(\\theta\\).\n\nFirst, recall the formula for the volume \\(V\\) of a tetrahedron formed by vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\):\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{6} \\left| \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) \\right|\n\\]\nSince the vectors are unit vectors, the volume can be expressed in terms of the scalar triple product and the angle \\(\\theta\\):\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{6} \\left| \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) \\right|\n\\]\nThe magnitude of the cross product \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\| = \\|\\mathbf{b}\\| \\|\\mathbf{c}\\| \\sin \\theta = \\sin \\theta\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) are unit vectors and the angle between any two of them is \\(\\theta\\), the scalar triple product \\(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\) can be simplified as:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\|\\mathbf{a}\\| \\|\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\| \\cos \\phi = \\sin \\theta \\cos \\phi\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\phi\\) is the angle between \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\). Since \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) form a regular tetrahedron, \\(\\phi = \\theta\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta\n\\]\n\nThus, the volume \\(V\\) becomes:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{6} \\left| \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\right|\n\\]\n\nGiven that the volume is \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{6} \\left| \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\right| = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\left| \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\right|\\):\n\\[\n\\left| \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\right| = \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{360}} = \\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\n\\]\n\nUsing the double-angle identity \\(\\sin 2\\theta = 2 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta\\):\n\\[\n\\left| \\sin 2\\theta \\right| = 2 \\left| \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\right| = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin 2\\theta = 2 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin 2\\theta = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta\\). Using the identity \\(\\cos 2\\theta = 2 \\cos^2 \\theta - 1\\), we can express \\(\\cos^2 \\theta\\) in terms of \\(\\cos 2\\theta\\):\n\\[\n\\cos^2 \\theta = \\frac{1 + \\cos 2\\theta}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe know:\n\\[\n\\cos 2\\theta = \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 2\\theta} = \\sqrt{1 - \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{2}{5}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{5}}\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n\\cos^2 \\theta = \\frac{1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{5}}}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{5} + \\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{5}}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we need to find:\n\\[\n3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity \\(\\cos^3 \\theta = \\cos \\theta (1 - \\sin^2 \\theta)\\), and simplifying, we find:\n\\[\n3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
